Helicobacter Pylori Infection
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Chronic dyspepsia, Epigastric burning pain, Halitosis, Positive urea breath test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-030
Reason for Visit: Exacerbation of deep, burning stomach pain radiating to the back, accompanied by nausea and passage of dark, foul-smelling stools.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a significant exacerbation of chronic burning epigastric pain accompanied by a report of passing dark, foul-smelling stools (suspected melaena). This constellation indicated a high risk for an acutely bleeding peptic ulcer secondary to long-standing infection. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to evaluate haemodynamic stability, perform a rectal examination, and determine the necessity for emergency hospital admission prior to standard consulting hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach has been burning for months, but today the pain is agonizing, I feel incredibly weak, and my stool was pitch black."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 6-month history of chronic persistent dyspepsia and epigastric discomfort. Describes a burning or gnawing upper abdominal pain occurring typically between meals or in the early hours of the morning, temporarily relieved by food or antacids. Reports nausea, occasional vomiting, and significant halitosis. Notes bloating and increased belching. Over the past 24 hours, the pain has become severe and constant. Patient reports passing two loose, black, tarry, and foul-smelling stools today. Associated history of chronic NSAID use for back pain.
Functional Status: Lying flat on the couch, acutely lethargic, feeling dizzy upon standing.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of significant gastrointestinal haemorrhage and hypovolaemia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 (Supine), 85/55 (Standing) | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, diaphoretic, and fatigued. Marked conjunctival pallor present.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is flat. Deep palpation reveals marked, localized epigastric tenderness without rigid board-like guarding or rebound peritonism (suggesting ulceration without free perforation). Bowel sounds are active. Digital rectal examination reveals characteristic melaena (black, tarry stool) on the examining glove. Orthostatic vital signs confirm significant intravascular volume depletion. The presentation is defined by chronic dyspepsia, epigastric burning pain, and halitosis; a positive urea breath test for H. pylori is presumed as the underlying driver of the now-bleeding ulcer.
Environmental Observations: Large bottle of over-the-counter Naproxen on the coffee table.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking daily NSAIDs; no current proton pump inhibitor coverage.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bleeding Peptic Ulcer Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Secondary to suspected H. pylori and NSAID use)
Differentials:

Gastric Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The classic history of pain relieved by eating and the absence of profound, unintentional weight loss or palpable metastatic nodes, though an endoscopy is required to definitively rule out malignancy.

Diverticular Haemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of melaena and severe epigastric pain, whereas diverticular bleeds are typically painless and present as bright red, fresh haematochezia from the lower bowel.

Peptic Ulcer Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The localized epigastric tenderness without the diffuse, board-like abdominal rigidity and absent bowel sounds characteristic of a free intra-abdominal rupture.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk is High)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute upper gastrointestinal bleed (melaena, orthostatic hypotension, tachycardia) superimposed on a chronic history typical of H. pylori-induced peptic ulcer disease exacerbated by NSAID use. The objective finding of melaena on digital rectal examination and symptomatic hypovolaemia mandated an immediate, urgent after-hours assessment. The patient is haemodynamically compromised and at high risk for catastrophic haemorrhagic shock. Delaying care until routine hours was absolutely contraindicated. Immediate escalation of care, including emergency ambulance transport for intravenous volume resuscitation and urgent endoscopic haemostasis, was the only safe clinical pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Placed the patient strictly nil by mouth (NBM). Contacted emergency services (000) for priority ambulance transport. Placed a large-bore IV cannula and commenced 1L Normal Saline stat on-site. Administered IV Pantoprazole 80mg (if available in emergency kit) to begin neutralizing acid.
Positive Behavioral Change: Remain completely flat on the couch and do not attempt to stand up under any circumstances. Keeping your body horizontal ensures that your limited remaining blood volume is physically directed by gravity to your brain and heart, preventing you from fainting and suffering a catastrophic head injury while we wait for the ambulance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Emergency hospital admission required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the patient vomits fresh red blood or coffee-ground material, or if they lose consciousness, the spouse must immediately update the 000 dispatcher to upgrade the priority.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a medical emergency requiring urgent hospital intervention to stop the bleeding.
Clinical Course: The hospital will perform an urgent camera test (endoscopy) to find the bleeding ulcer and clip or burn it shut. Later, you will be tested and treated for a specific stomach bacteria (H. pylori) that likely caused the ulcer.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk to the bathroom or sit upright. The paramedics will move you carefully using a specialized stretcher to keep your blood pressure from dropping further.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A combination of a chronic bacterial infection and your regular painkiller use has eroded a deep ulcer into the lining of your stomach, which has now struck a blood vessel and is actively bleeding. The black stool is digested blood passing through your bowel. The hospital's immediate priority is to replace your lost fluids and physically seal the bleeding vessel from the inside using a small camera.
Actionable Steps: Completely and permanently stop taking Naproxen or any other anti-inflammatory medications immediately. These drugs actively destroy the protective mucus layer in your stomach; taking them with an active ulcer is directly pouring acid onto an open, bleeding wound.

